---
// D2–D3: nature uses the same recipe as the Life-like network automata just before (a flock, a brain); the rule is the
// genotype, the behaviour the phenotype, and the thesis wants to narrow the gap between them.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import { phi } from './tex';
// D3: the genotype's two rings, in design pixels of their canvas (src/scripts/defence/donut.ts draws them)
const rings = [{ x: 185, y: 150, R: 135, w: 56, on: false }, { x: 535, y: 150, R: 135, w: 56, on: true }];
---
<Slide label="Nature does this too: a flock" builds={2} class="d2 d2-flock">
  <h2>Nature does this too</h2>
  <div class="d2-row">
    <figure>
      <p class="lede">Boids and your mouse</p>
      <div class="d2-box">
        <canvas class="boids" aria-label="A flock of birds, each following three local rules; the pointer scares them"></canvas>
        <SimButton class="d2-btn" />
      </div>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <p class="lede">Starlings and a falcon</p>
      <div class="d2-box">
        <video src="/demos/defence/media/starlings.webm" muted loop playsinline preload="auto" aria-label="A starling flock splitting around a peregrine falcon and closing up behind it"></video>
        <SimButton class="d2-btn" />
      </div>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">Nature is full of the same recipe: simple local rules, on a network of who reacts to whom. Every bird in a flock follows a few local rules: align with your neighbours, stay close, don’t collide. Two birds are easy to understand; the flock as a whole, turning and splitting and closing up again around a threat, is not. The flock on the left is made of boids, the simulated birds of Craig Reynolds (1987), each following just those rules. Move the mouse over them: they scatter from it and close up behind it.</p>
  <p slot="say">The same happens in the sky over Rome: a starling flock splits around a peregrine falcon (the small dark bird near the gap) and closes up again behind it. Then, in one long shot, the falcon chases the flock, and dark waves run through it as the birds swerve away, one after the other. Nobody in the flock sees the whole; each bird reacts only to the birds nearest to it. (Video: C. Hemelrijk et al. 2019, from the videos to “Damping of waves of agitation in starling flocks”, CC BY 4.0; two clips joined and cropped.)</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="Nature does this too: a brain" builds={2} class="d2 d2-brain">
  <h2>Nature does this too</h2>
  <div class="d2-row">
    <figure>
      <p class="lede">Neurons firing</p>
      <div class="d2-box">
        <canvas class="brain" aria-label="A network of neurons in the shape of a brain, firing"></canvas>
        <SimButton class="d2-btn" />
      </div>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <p class="lede">Experiencing and steering reality</p>
      <div class="d2-box">
        <video src="/demos/defence/media/octopus.webm" muted loop playsinline preload="auto" aria-label="An octopus asleep in its den: its skin darkens, breaks into blotches and pales again"></video>
        <SimButton class="d2-btn" />
      </div>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">A single neuron is well understood: it fires, or not, depending on its inputs. How billions of them together experience reality and steer through it, and even dream, is one of the hardest questions there is. The brain on the left is only an illustration: a neuron fires when enough of its inputs fired, then rests for a moment.</p>
  <p slot="say">On the right, an octopus fast asleep. Every so often a sleeping octopus slips into a short phase of active sleep, and its skin comes alive: it darkens, breaks into blotches and pales again, running through the same patterns it uses when it is awake, to hide or to signal. That skin is a screen of tiny colour cells, each opened and closed by nerves from the brain, so what we are watching is the brain at work, with nothing outside to react to. The researchers found that its brain activity in these moments looks like waking activity, and compare it to our own REM sleep, the sleep in which we dream. Nobody knows what an octopus experiences; but simple cells, wired together, produce something that looks remarkably like a dream. (Video: an octopus in active sleep, A. Pophale et al., Nature 2023, CC BY 4.0; cropped and trimmed.)</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="Genotype and phenotype" builds={3} class="d3">
  <div class="d3-body">
  <div class="gp">
    <div class="col center">
      <h3>Genotype: the rule</h3>
      <p>local, simple<br />what each node does</p>
      <canvas class="geno" data-rings={JSON.stringify(rings)} aria-label="The rule as two rings: where a node that is off is born, and where a node that is on survives"></canvas>
      <p class="d3-phi" set:html={phi(9, 170, 170, true)} />
    </div>
    <div class="arrows" aria-hidden="true">⇄</div>
    <div class="col center">
      <h3>Phenotype: the behaviour</h3>
      <p>global, rich<br />what the whole does</p>
      <div class="d3-box">
        <canvas class="pheno" aria-label="The rule on the left, running on a random network of 150 nodes"></canvas>
        <SimButton class="d3-btn" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="rq" data-b="2">
    <p class="rq-label">The research question</p>
    <p class="q">What links a simple local rule to the rich behaviour it produces, <span data-b="3">and can we put that link to use?</span></p>
  </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">Borrowing two words from biology (after S. A. Kauffman, 1990): the rule is the <em>genotype</em>, local and simple, what each node does; the behaviour is the <em>phenotype</em>, global and rich, what the whole does. Here the rule is Replicator, <Fragment set:html={phi(9, 170, 170)} />, on the random network of 150 nodes from before, started with a click: it never settles. The rule fits in two rings, and yet you cannot read what the network does off those rings. On this page, click the rings to change the rule while it runs.</p>
  <p slot="say">That gap is what the thesis is about, and a Life-like network automaton is a good place to study it: the rule is as simple as a rule gets, the network can be anything, and every step can be watched. Its research question has two halves: what links a simple local rule to the rich behaviour it produces, and can we put that link to use? The rule never tells the whole story, but it tells more than nothing, and the behaviour in turn tells us something about the rule and about the network it runs on.</p>
</Slide>

<style>

  .d2-row { display: flex; gap: calc(64 * var(--u)); flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .d2-row figure { margin: 0; flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: calc(24 * var(--u)); }
  .d2-row .lede { padding-top: calc(52 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(42 * var(--u)); } /* one line, just above its picture */
  .d2-box { position: relative; }
  .d2-row canvas, .d2-row video { display: block; width: calc(832 * var(--u)); height: calc(586 * var(--u)); background: var(--panel); }
  .d2-row video { object-fit: contain; }
  .d2-box :global(.d2-btn) { right: calc(16 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(16 * var(--u)); }
  .boids { touch-action: none; }

  .d3-body { flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; gap: calc(28 * var(--u)); padding-bottom: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .gp { display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: flex-start; gap: calc(50 * var(--u)); }
  .gp > .col { width: calc(720 * var(--u)); gap: calc(20 * var(--u)); }
  .gp h3 { font-size: calc(50 * var(--u)); }
  .arrows { font-size: calc(120 * var(--u)); color: var(--accent); align-self: center; line-height: 1; }
  .geno { width: calc(720 * var(--u)); height: calc(300 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(6 * var(--u)); }
  .d3-phi { font-size: calc(58 * var(--u)); }
  .d3-box { position: relative; }
  .pheno { width: calc(460 * var(--u)); height: calc(460 * var(--u)); }
  .d3-box :global(.d3-btn) { right: calc(-84 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(0 * var(--u)); }
  .rq { margin-top: calc(20 * var(--u)); text-align: center; }
  .rq-label { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: calc(10 * var(--u)); }
  .q { font-weight: 700; font-size: calc(62 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.2; max-width: calc(1600 * var(--u)); margin: 0 auto; }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim, fresh } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker, pointer } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { makeRng } from '../../scripts/net';
  import { step, randomState, type Rule } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { picker, setPhi, type Ring } from '../../scripts/defence/donut';
  import { picture, drawNet, type Picture } from '../../scripts/defence/netdraw';
  import { trioNet, trioStart, RHO0 } from '../../scripts/defence/trio';
  import { flock, stepFlock } from '../../scripts/defence/boids';
  import { brain, fire, outline, FOLDS, BOX } from '../../scripts/defence/brain';

  /** A clip beside a simulation: the same ▶/↺ button, started by its own build. */
  function clip(s: HTMLElement, k: number) {
    const v = s.querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>('video')!, btn = s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')[1];
    // a seek decodes a frame, which can stall a slow machine, so rewind only when the clip has moved
    sim(btn, k, { reset() { if (!v.paused) v.pause(); if (v.currentTime > 0) v.currentTime = 0; }, play() { v.play().catch(() => {}); } });
  }

  // ── D2: a flock that scatters from the pointer (build 1) beside starlings around a falcon (build 2) ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.d2-flock')) {
    const c = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.boids')!;
    const W = 832, H = 586, BIRDS = 100;
    let f = flock(BIRDS, W, H, makeRng(1987)), seed = 1987, avoid: { x: number; y: number } | null = null;
    const paintFlock = () => {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, k = st.w / W;
      ctx.fillStyle = t.ink;
      for (let i = 0; i < f.n; i++) {
        const a = Math.atan2(f.vy[i], f.vx[i]), x = f.x[i] * k, y = f.y[i] * k;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(x + Math.cos(a) * 14, y + Math.sin(a) * 14);
        ctx.lineTo(x + Math.cos(a + 2.5) * 8, y + Math.sin(a + 2.5) * 8);
        ctx.lineTo(x + Math.cos(a - 2.5) * 8, y + Math.sin(a - 2.5) * 8);
        ctx.fill();
      }
    };
    const birds = ticker(s, 50, () => stepFlock(f, avoid), paintFlock);
    const at = (e: PointerEvent) => { avoid = pointer(c, e); }; // the canvas is W design pixels wide: flock coordinates
    c.addEventListener('pointermove', at);
    c.addEventListener('pointerdown', at);
    c.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => { avoid = null; });
    sim(s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!, 1, {
      reset() { birds.play(false); f = flock(BIRDS, W, H, makeRng(seed)); avoid = null; paintFlock(); },
      play() { birds.play(true); },
      reroll() { seed = (Math.random() * 4294967296) >>> 0; },
    });
    clip(s, 2);
    slide(s, { leave() { birds.play(false); } });
    onSize(c, paintFlock);
  }

  // ── D2b: a brain whose neurons fire (build 1) beside an octopus in active sleep (build 2) ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.d2-brain')) {
    const b = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.brain')!;
    // the brain: 0 resting, 1 firing, 2–3 recovering; `glow` fades each neuron after it fired
    const net = brain(260, 28, 6, 12, makeRng(3)), poly = outline(), REST = 3;
    let st0 = new Uint8Array(net.n), st1 = new Uint8Array(net.n), rnd = makeRng(9), seed = 9;
    const glow = new Float32Array(net.n);
    const warm = () => { st0.fill(0); glow.fill(0); rnd = makeRng(seed); for (let t = 0; t < 40; t++) { fire(net, st0, st1, 0.25, REST, 0.003, rnd); [st0, st1] = [st1, st0]; } };
    const paintBrain = () => {
      const st = stage(b); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, k = Math.min(st.w / BOX.w, st.h / BOX.h) * 0.96, ox = (st.w - BOX.w * k) / 2, oy = (st.h - BOX.h * k) / 2;
      const X = (i: number) => ox + net.xy[2 * i] * k, Y = (i: number) => oy + net.xy[2 * i + 1] * k;
      ctx.lineJoin = 'round';
      ctx.beginPath(); poly.forEach(([x, y], i) => (i ? ctx.lineTo(ox + x * k, oy + y * k) : ctx.moveTo(ox + x * k, oy + y * k))); ctx.closePath();
      ctx.fillStyle = t.bg; ctx.fill(); ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 5; ctx.stroke();
      ctx.lineWidth = 4; ctx.lineCap = 'round';
      for (const fold of FOLDS) { ctx.beginPath(); fold.forEach(([x, y], i) => (i ? ctx.lineTo(ox + x * k, oy + y * k) : ctx.moveTo(ox + x * k, oy + y * k))); ctx.stroke(); }
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.2; ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.beginPath();
      for (const [i, j] of net.edges) { ctx.moveTo(X(i), Y(i)); ctx.lineTo(X(j), Y(j)); }
      ctx.stroke();
      // a signal along each link out of a neuron that just fired
      ctx.strokeStyle = t.accent; ctx.lineWidth = 2.4; ctx.globalAlpha = 0.55; ctx.beginPath();
      for (const [i, j] of net.edges) if ((st0[i] === 1 && st0[j] === 2) || (st0[j] === 1 && st0[i] === 2)) { ctx.moveTo(X(i), Y(i)); ctx.lineTo(X(j), Y(j)); }
      ctx.stroke(); ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
      for (let i = 0; i < net.n; i++) {
        const g = glow[i];
        if (g > 0.02) { ctx.globalAlpha = 0.25 * g; ctx.fillStyle = t.accent; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(X(i), Y(i), 17, 0, 7); ctx.fill(); }
        ctx.globalAlpha = 1; ctx.fillStyle = g > 0.02 ? t.accent : t.muted;
        ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(X(i), Y(i), 4.5 + 3.5 * g, 0, 7); ctx.fill();
      }
      ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
    };
    // firing at six rounds a second; the glow decays every frame in between
    let last = 0;
    const neurons = ticker(s, 60, () => {
      const now = performance.now();
      for (let i = 0; i < net.n; i++) glow[i] *= 0.9;
      if (now - last >= 160) { last = now; fire(net, st0, st1, 0.25, REST, 0.003, rnd); [st0, st1] = [st1, st0]; for (let i = 0; i < net.n; i++) if (st0[i] === 1) glow[i] = 1; }
    }, paintBrain);
    sim(s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!, 1, {
      reset() { neurons.play(false); warm(); paintBrain(); },
      play() { neurons.play(true); },
      reroll() { seed = (Math.random() * 4294967296) >>> 0; },
    });
    clip(s, 2);
    slide(s, { leave() { neurons.play(false); } });
    warm();
    onSize(b, paintBrain);
  }

  // ── D3: the genotype, Replicator as two rings at resolution 9 (clickable, donut.ts), beside its phenotype, the rule
  // running on the talk's random network (trio.ts, slide F3), started at build 1: it never settles. A click on the rings
  // changes the rule as it runs. The first start is the network's own, as on F3; a reset draws a new one. The network is
  // laid out when first shown. ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.d3')) {
    const rc = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.geno')!, nc = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.pheno')!;
    const label = s.querySelector('.d3-phi')!, REPLICATOR = { r: 9, B: 170, S: 170 };
    let pic: Picture | null = null, rule: Rule = { ...REPLICATOR }, first: Uint8Array | null = null, a = new Uint8Array(0), b = a;
    const net = () => {
      if (!pic) { pic = picture(trioNet(0), 460, 22, 6.5); first = trioStart(0, pic.net.n); a = first.slice(); b = new Uint8Array(pic.net.n); }
      return pic;
    };
    const paint = () => { const st = stage(nc); if (st) drawNet(st, net(), a); };
    const run = ticker(nc, 5, () => { step(a, net().net, rule, b); [a, b] = [b, a]; }, paint);
    const rules = picker(rc, JSON.parse(rc.dataset.rings!) as [Ring, Ring], REPLICATOR, (x) => { rule = x; setPhi(label, x); });
    sim(s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!, 1, {
      reset() { run.play(false); net(); a = first!.slice(); paint(); },
      play() { run.play(true); },
      reroll() { first = randomState(net().net.n, RHO0, fresh()); },
    });
    slide(s, {
      enter() { rules.reset(REPLICATOR); first = trioStart(0, net().net.n); },
      leave() { run.play(false); },
    });
    onSize(nc, paint);
  }
</script>
